2.0.0 (2026):

64-bit port (LP64, tested on x86_64 Linux) and robustness work for
ncacn_ip_tcp servers and clients.  The version number changes because the
ABI changed.

- The RPC runtime, the NDR marshaling code and the IDL compiler work on
  LP64 (pointer and long sizes, alignment, union discriminants, full
  pointers, format strings).
- Builds with current toolchains (C23 IDL compiler; tested with gcc 14 to
  16, autoconf 2.71 to 2.73 on Ubuntu 26.04, Debian 13, AlmaLinux 10 and
  Arch Linux), in tree and out of tree, parallel make; "make check" in
  dcethreads, the perf suite and the new load test (tests/loadtest).
- dcethreads delivers DCE cancels itself instead of through NPTL
  cancellation (DCETHREADS_CANCEL=nptl selects the old code); lost wakeup
  in the condition wait fixed.
- The UUID functions are part of libdcerpc (there is no DCE libuuid any
  more; do not link -luuid); libdcerpc symbols are versioned DCERPC_1.1.
- Connections: poll listener without the FD_SETSIZE limit, accept backoff,
  immediate restart of servers with a well-known endpoint, context rundown
  for killed clients and vanished client hosts, idle connection reclaim
  (RPC_CN_SERVER_IDLE_TIMEOUT), client bind timeout, per-connection
  keepalive and user timeout (RPC_CN_KEEPALIVE, RPC_CN_USER_TIMEOUT).
- Many runtime fixes found by the tests, ASan and valgrind (maybe calls,
  liveness monitoring lock, association group reference counts, status
  exceptions, UDP cancels).

See README for the build and the runtime settings.

DCOM:

We (well, me mainly) are beginning to support DCOM/Object RPC as documented by Microsoft.

The aim is to produce a free implementation of DCOM that can be installed by
anyone on anyone's Linux box without a heavy license fee.  People can then
really think about using it for their applications and then we might find a
whole host of component-orientated applications start to spring up, and then
we might find a lot of components being created to make the applications
easier to develop, and the range of applications will grow and grow...

For more info on this, subscribe to the mailing list:
	freedce-dcomdev@lists.sourceforge.net

